# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build context exclusions.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is not an optimisation — it is what keeps secrets and gigabytes out
# of the build. Two entries below are load-bearing:
#
#   .env / .env.local   Docker sends the whole context to the daemon, and
#                       `COPY . .` puts every file in a layer. Anything matched
#                       here never enters an image, so a stray .env cannot leak
#                       into a layer that gets pushed to a registry.
#   node_modules        ~400 MB on a Windows host. The image builds its own
#                       copy inside Linux; the host's is incompatible (native
#                       binaries for the wrong platform) and would silently
#                       overwrite the correct one on `COPY . .`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Secrets — never in an image
.env
.env.*
!.env.production.example
*.pem
*.key

# providers.json is a LIVE CREDENTIAL FILE, not a config template.
#
# It is the legacy single-user provider store, and the copy in this working
# directory holds three real, unencrypted API keys (an OmniRoute gateway key, an
# APINeX key and a Google key). `.env*` above does not match it, so before this
# line it was copied into the build context and baked into the image by
# `COPY . .` — where `docker history` and a `docker save | tar -x` would both
# hand it to anyone who could pull the image.
#
# Nothing in the image needs it. `importLegacyProvidersFor()` reads it once, per
# user, to carry a pre-existing local setup into the per-user `user_providers`
# table; on a fresh server there is nothing to carry over, and a server that did
# import it would give every account the operator's own keys.
providers.json

# Same class of mistake with a different name. The settings panel writes this
# file, so it exists on any machine the app has run on, and `.env.*` does not
# match a name that does not START with `.env`.
user_local.env
*.env
!.env.production.example

# Local database and its write-ahead log. This is the live data file; it must
# never be baked into an image, and including it would make every build
# non-deterministic.
chat.db
chat.db-wal
chat.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# The timestamped copies the in-app backup path leaves behind. These are named
# `chat.db.backup-1789485009526`, which neither `chat.db` nor the `*backup_*.*`
# pattern further down matches — five of them, 10 MB each, were being uploaded
# to the Docker daemon and written into a layer on every single build.
chat.db.backup-*
chat.db.*

# Runtime state for the Deep Cowork context layer, including workspace.json,
# which records an absolute path on the developer's own machine. Regenerated on
# demand; meaningless (and misleading) inside a container.
.omniroute
.agent

# Windows launchers, one-off repair scripts and schema dumps. Developer tooling
# that the server never executes, and `fix-agentrouter-config.sql` embeds
# provider configuration that should not travel.
*.bat
check-schema.js
run-fix.js
fix-*.js
fix-*.sql
activate-*.js

# Dependencies and build output — rebuilt inside the image
node_modules
**/node_modules
.next
out
build
dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# (The database, its WAL and its timestamped copies are excluded up in the
# secrets block, alongside providers.json — they are the same class of problem:
# live data that must not enter a layer.)

# Documents the app generated for users at runtime. `public/` is copied into the
# final image wholesale, so without this every PDF exported during local testing
# ships inside the image — and `public/` is served publicly, meaning one user's
# document would be downloadable by anyone who guessed the filename. Runtime
# output does not belong in a build artefact. Safe to exclude: pdf-generator.ts
# recreates the directory with mkdirSync(recursive) before writing.
public/generated-pdfs

# The path the LIVE route actually writes to. `public/generated-pdfs` above is
# used by src/lib/pdf-generator.ts; src/app/api/chat/generate-document/route.ts
# — the one page.tsx calls — writes to <cwd>/generated-documents instead, which
# was not excluded. Same reasoning applies: runtime output, one directory shared
# by every account, and no reason for a single byte of it to enter an image
# layer. The route calls fs.mkdir(recursive) before writing, so excluding it
# cannot break a cold start.
generated-documents

# Backups the app writes, and the manual backups kept alongside source files.
#
# The `**/` prefixes matter. A .dockerignore pattern is matched against the path
# relative to the ROOT of the build context and `*` does not cross a `/`, so the
# bare `*_backup.*` form below only ever matched a file sitting next to this
# one. Every real copy is nested — `src/lib/omniroute_backup.ts`,
# `src/app/api/chat/route_backup.ts` and six more inside src/app/api — so none
# of them was actually being excluded, and all of them were being uploaded to
# the daemon and baked into a layer by `COPY . .`. That is ~3,000 lines of
# superseded handler code, including copies from before the routes were
# guarded, sitting inside a distributable image.
#
# Kept narrow for the same reason as in .gitignore: `**/*backup*` would also
# swallow src/components/BackupsPanel.tsx, which is live code, and the build
# would fail on a missing import rather than warn.
.omniroute-backups
*_backup.*
*backup_*.*
**/*_backup.*
**/*backup_*.*
**/backup_*.*
**/smart-backup*
src/app/api/omniroute/debug/reout)backup.ts

# Version control and editor state
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store

# Docs and dev tooling that the server never reads
README.md
*.md
eslint.config.mjs
.vscode-extension
vscode-extension

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
